Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

## Summary

- add a README describing the repository as the shared source of truth for reusable agent skills;
- add a Windows installer that creates one directory junction per skill under `~/.agents/skills` by default;
- add a macOS/Linux installer that creates one symlink per skill under `~/.agents/skills` by default;
- preserve unrelated locally installed skills and refuse to overwrite collisions;
- document alternate target directories, updates via `git pull`, and machine-specific configuration guidance.

## Notes

The installers link each direct subdirectory under `skills/` individually instead of replacing the whole skill store. This keeps repository-managed skills compatible with local-only skills.

Part of #695 (PR A: the API). Part of #680, step 3.

## Why

A send named a draft only by its id. A draft can change in place after the person saw it, through `set_letter_words`, `set_stationery`, `set_letter_signature`, `set_arrival_date`, `set_mail_service` and `set_postcard_style`, from the chat or the card. It was then mailed as changed. The person should send what they saw, or nothing.

## What changes

- **The content version** ([contentVersion.ts](src/services/contentVersion.ts)): 16 hex characters of a SHA-256 over every column that prints or decides delivery, read from the stored row. That covers:
  - the words, pages and layout;
  - the stationery, design included;
  - the signature, header, inline and front pictures (by hash);
  - the mail service and both dates;
  - a postcard's size and front;
  - both addresses;
  - the gift flag and the renderer.

  The preview's HTML and the price are not part of it.
- **Who gives it:**
  - every preview (`createDraft`/`createPostcardDraft` `RETURNING`);
  - every editor (the version it wrote, read in its own transaction through `onWritten`);
  - `get_draft_status` while ready;
  - the confirmation page's `GET /api/sends/:draftId`.

  The editors and `get_draft_status` hash pictures in the database. The send, the checkout and the page hash them in Node. The two agree, as a postgres test proves, characters beyond ASCII included.
- **Who takes it back:**
  - `send_letter`, `send_postcard` and `create_mail_checkout` (an optional `contentVersion`);
  - the page's `POST /api/sends/:draftId` and `/checkout`. A non-string version gets `400 invalid_content_version`.
- **The comparison:**
  - The prepaid send compares under the draft lock, before any letter is spent.
  - The checkout compares under the draft lock, before any order or Stripe session.
  - A version that differs is refused as `DRAFT_CHANGED_SINCE_SHOWN`. The tools tell the model to show the mail again. The page answers `409 changed_since_shown`.
  - **For one release a missing version is allowed and logged** (`send.content_version_missing`, `checkout.content_version_missing`), while the cards and the website come to send it. A later PR makes it required.
- **The editor lock:** no editor changes a draft whose Pay & Send order is live. A checkout whose Stripe session opened counts as live until Stripe closes it (its webhook or the sweep), whatever our own window says. So the paid fulfilment mails what was paid for. Every editor's refusal now gives the way on.
- **Schemas:** input and output fields; the served-tools snapshot and `manifest.json` regenerated.
- **Docs:** [letter-send-flow.md](docs/letter-send-flow.md) ("What the Person Saw") and [tool-apis.md](docs/tool-apis.md).

Next, in their own PRs: the cards send the version they drew and redraw on refusal; website #63 does the same on the confirmation page; then the version becomes required.

## Tests

- **Unit:**
  - `contentVersion` (every field, picture hashes from data and from the database, dates, defaults, matching);
  - the send and checkout refusals, including a locked-read proof;
  - the editors' `onWritten` (told on success, never on a refusal);
  - the six editor tools and the previews returning it;
  - the API's GET and POST, refusals and the 400;
  - schemas.
- **Postgres:**
  - `signatureDrafts.postgres.test.ts`: every reader gives one version; an edit reports the version it wrote; the send refuses the version shown before the edit and takes the one after.
  - `arriveBy.postgres.test.ts`: an order whose session opened holds the draft past its window.

## Review

Cold Opus reviews (sending and money: two clean rounds needed):

- Review round 1: changes. Medium: once our checkout window passed, the editors could change a draft whose Stripe session could still be paid. Fixed in 679dece by holding the draft until Stripe closes the session. Lows also fixed:
  - blank versions read as none;
  - the input description names only tools the model can call;
  - the unused reader is removed;
  - a doc line corrected.
- Review round 2: merge. Lows folded into fb4b6d9:
  - every editor's refusal gives the way on;
  - the refusals name only tools the model can call;
  - the closing-checkout comment and doc are corrected;
  - the checkout's locked-read test.

  Two older Lows are filed as #721: one predicate for a live order across the send, duplicate check and editors; and the sweep giving up on an unreadable session.
- Review round 3: merge. Lows folded into 212442a (comments, docs and one exact test): the version's docs say it covers every column an in-place edit can change and every one that decides delivery, and that a new editor must add what it writes; the lock's comment names the open session; get_draft_status's version is pinned to the row it read. Not done: a postcard run in the postgres agreement test (the logic is shared). Sweep starvation is added to #721.

Mutants: 34/34 killed on fb4b6d9 (`C:/letter-irl-scripts/mutate-695.mjs` with `mutate-695-mutants.mjs`). The commits after it change comments, docs and one test, and merge origin/dev. The mutants cover the hash (each field, picture hashes, empty pictures, dates, trimming, missing as a match), the send and checkout refusals and their logging, the page (GET, both POSTs, the 400, both refusals), the editors (`onWritten` told and not told, the schedule pass-through, the open-session lock, the refusal's way on), `createDraft`/`createPostcardDraft`, the three send tools, the checkout wording, `get_draft_status`, `set_stationery` and both previews.

## To check on DEV after merge

Refresh the ChatGPT (DEV) connector. Then:
1. Preview a letter, change its stationery from the card, and send from the card. It sends, because the cards do not send a version yet and a missing one is allowed.
2. Call `send_letter` with a stale `contentVersion` through the DEV CLI connector. It is refused with `DRAFT_CHANGED_SINCE_SHOWN` and nothing is spent.

CI: see checks.

